A||N||31||CoolBlue87GT||Shopping for digital camara.  Any advice ?||||5||
Z||000000||CoolBlue87GT||06-05-2001||09:25 AM||||With all the digital photo's I've seen, I'm in the market to buy one.<P>With so many styles out there, what would be a good brand/type to get ? Price ?<P>I guess one that had a zoom feature would be good. Are there variable zoom settings,<BR>or fixed on most ?<P>Thanks for any information.<BR>.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/CoolBlue87GT.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||WampusCat||06-05-2001||09:52 AM||||I've got an Olympus C2100 Ultra Zoom.  2.1 megapixel with 10X zoom.  It's equivalent to a 38-380mm lens on a 35mm.  It's got image stabilization to prevent blurring on high power zoom, built in flash, and USB cable for speedy downloads so you won't need a card reader.  $699 from cameraworld.com||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||06-05-2001||09:53 AM||||Hey CB!  I bought one not long ago.  I was thinking of a camera, but went with a Sony DCR-TRV720 NTSC Digital Handycam instead.  Not only is it a digital camera, but it takes movies too.  You can also take still shots on the digital tape making it like a slide show with sound.  They have come down in price a little too.  Having the ability to make a movie or a still shot for about the same price as a good digital camera got me to fork out the extra bucks to get the camera with both.  The movie camera has a digital tape and the still camera has a memory stick.  Well there a little more than my 2 cents. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  There's pretty good info. on the web.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||10:21 AM||||Ive got my eye on one at BJ's - panasonic 1280 x 960, 3x optical zoom, lcd on the back about $240. (dont know the model #)<P>  Im hoping they will drop to about $200 soon.<P>  One thing to look for - digital zoom verse optical zoom - in my opinion the optical zoom is real - the digital zoom is simply taking the image and making it bigger by making the pixels bigger or by throwing part of the image away - optics is where its at.<P>  you can get a snap shot 640 x 480 camera for around $80 now - RCA has one thats ok.<P>   all depends on what you want to do with it I guess.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||10:23 AM||||BTW zoom means variable - I havent seen any digital cameras with fixed telephoto or wide angle settings that can be selected.<P>  the CCD (the thing that catches the image) on a digital camera is so small that making zoom lenses for them is very easy.||||reg||1
Z||000005||CoolBlue87GT||06-05-2001||10:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>BTW zoom means variable - I havent seen any digital cameras with fixed telephoto </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The RCA camara I've seen, indeed has only two settings, normal & zoom.<P>I'd like to find one that I can select how far to zoom in, (not by walking backwards or forwards)<P>My 35mm has extra lens that goes from 70 to 205 zoom (and anywhere in between)<P>Now I know digital camara's won't have that, but I think you may understand what I mean now.<P>Thanks WampusCat, stsmithgt, & Ken Wiffief||||reg||1
Z||000006||WampusCat||06-05-2001||11:37 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B><BR>My 35mm has extra lens that goes from 70 to 205 zoom (and anywhere in between)<P>Now I know digital camara's won't have that, but I think you may understand what I mean now.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The C-2100 optically zooms from 38-380mm and anywhere in between.  It also has a 2.7 digital zoom after you reach the limit of the optical zoom, but like Ken said, you lose image quality with the digital zoom. You can use a 1.7X teleconverter to get 650mm but you lose low-light capability and you need a steady tripod. They do make digital cameras that use interchangeable lenses like a 35mm SLR but they are mega pricey.  Canon EOS D30 is about $3000 for body only.<P>check out <A HREF="http://www.dpreview.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dpreview.com/</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.megapixel.net/html/issueindex.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.megapixel.net/html/issueindex.html</A>   <A HREF="http://www.imaging-resource.com/DIGCAM01.HTM" TARGET=_blank>http://www.imaging-resource.com/DIGCAM01.HTM</A>  for lots of info<BR><p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||11:44 AM||||I think you are right - digital zoom probabally is either here or there, not in between.<P>  all the optical zooms I have seen on any camera or camcorder are always real zooms, infinitely adjustable.<P> another good reason to look for optical zoom and ignore digital zoom.||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||06-05-2001||12:32 PM||||Kodak has some really nice inexpensive units. I have a DC280 3x digital 2x optical zoom. I got it about 2 years ago for 500 and I'm sure its "OLD" by todays standards. When the reviews came out for this camera it was ranked as one of the best bang for the buck. I will be getting a new unit soon most likely a Kodak 4800 or 5000. <BR>Yeah ignore the digital zoom. Digital zoom is the same thing a computer program does when you do a zoom in on a picture.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||Alex4mula||06-05-2001||02:42 PM||||I bought an RCA DS1000 or something at Walmart. It is a basic 640x480 with serial, USB and TV cables. Cost was $89+tax. Photos were ok for web and email. I took some pictures and returned it becasue the S/W didn't work with Windows NT.||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||06-05-2001||03:31 PM||||Dave check out price watch and click on electronic on the left side then on Digital prices range fro 19.00 to 545.00<BR> <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pricewatch.com/</A> <P>||||reg||14
Z||000011||cadaver||06-05-2001||11:09 PM||||I have two sony mavica's. I really like this camera as it takes a regular 3.5 disk. You dont have to mess with links and docking stations. Just put the disk in your a drive. It will also copy a disk so you can give a copy to someone at the time. Not download them and send them.||||reg||1
Z||000012||CoolBlue87GT||06-05-2001||11:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR>[B]I have two sony mavica's. B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What was the price ?  How many pic's to a disk ?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000013||PeaceDawg||06-05-2001||11:38 PM||||I have a Sony Mavica too and I love it...nothing is more convenient.  Can fit about 35 pics to one floppy...good pic quality and 10X zoom...<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000014||FieroHarry||06-05-2001||11:42 PM||||No watch this,<BR>I got a 64mb memory stick for my mavica, took 382 pictures at carlisle with room for 1/3 more.<BR>Along with my 5 hour battery and im combat ready baby! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 264K+ <I>FOCOSEVA,FOCOA</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B>||||reg||1
Z||000015||CoolBlue87GT||06-06-2001||12:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroHarry:<BR><B>No watch this,<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>HOW MUCH !!!  ???<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||GarethWright||06-06-2001||01:14 AM||||Dad just invested in a Canon Powershot G1, 3.3 megapixel, 2048x1536, 1024x768 and 640x480 resolution, 4x Digital zoom and 3x optical zoom (7-21mm), comes with a 16mb card, USB connector, software, battery, charger, neckstrap etc.  Best Buy $799.95<BR>It's a really great camera, but for that price, you could get a good start on a 3.4 conversion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Alex4mula||06-06-2001||10:09 AM||||Sony Mavicas starts at near $400 for the 640x480 in floppy to over $900 for the 2.2Mp in CD-R. They are convenient but big. Can't have it all...||||reg||1
Z||000018||CoolBlue87GT||06-06-2001||10:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GarethWright:<BR><B>Best Buy $799.95 It's a really great camera, but for that price, you could get a good start on a 3.4 conversion   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>WHAT !  A 3.4 conversion ??  On a 100% stock Fiero ??   AHHHHWWWWWW  NO NO NO NO<P>Now I'm going to have nightmears !!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>That Camara sound interesting.<P>All the replies tell me one thing, Optical zoom is the way to go, but many different type of camaras to choose from.<P>I'll take this list & not waste any time looking at "junk" thats not on the list.<P>Digital zoom should knock out at lease half on the camaras in the stores / internet stores<P>I do plan to make sure which ever I choose, it must use "AA" batterys.  In my store, we have a small charger that plugs into the car lighter, and recharges in half them time as the home chargers. One does it in about an hour<P>Well, I have some good information to start with.  I'd hate to walk into a camara store and say duh, um, I'd like a digital camara, but um,  don't know anything about them.<P>A saleperson just loves people like that, and say, here a piece of crap that will make me a nice profit,-- Oh I mean this is a good one !<P>Thanks all for the info.  Looks like online buying might be better. Thank Bill84, I've been looking at that site <A HREF="http://www.pricewatch.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.pricewatch.com/</A>  <P>Catch U all later.  Will watch for any other information.   Thanks,   Dave<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||stsmithgt||06-06-2001||11:26 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.epinions.com/elec-Video-Camcorders-All-Sony_DCR-TRV720" TARGET=_blank>http://www.epinions.com/elec-Video-Camcorders-All-Sony_DCR-TRV720</A> <P>Check this address out to see the digital Camera/Video camera I bought. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>It has some reviews and info..  I think they're running about 800 bucks now.  I paid more, but I didn't want to wait for the price to come down last year.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||AusFiero||06-06-2001||11:44 AM||||The Sony Mavicas are a great camera for all round ease of use etc. And good quality. I wish mine never got stolen.<P>Got a palm size Panasonic NV-DS33 digital video now. Who says digital zoom is no good? I have 100X digital zoom on this camera and it kicks ass. Perhaps it is no good on cheaper cameras but it works nice on this camera. There was not much change out of $3000 here. Think they are about $1300 US now.<P>Would give you a whole lot of resons to keep the car stock with that empty a wallet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000021||ferg||06-06-2001||11:52 AM||||I have a sony dsc-s30. I paid about $500 a year ago, I think they've come down. It comes with a 4mb memory stick, using the 640x480 setting I get approx. 40 pics on it. I bought a carry case as well which gave me an extra battery and an 8mb memory stick. The batteries hold the charge really well, and the recharger is pretty portable too. I've had a digital camera that used AA, they didn't last nearly as long as this sony battery. <P>Downloading the pics is easy, you can use the usb, or parallel ports. <P>There are tons of things this camera can do, like take B&W, solarized, antiqued (yellowish-brown pics), you can adust the focus depth. It has an optical zoom, built in flash, lcd screen (that you can see outside). <P>I think it takes great pictures, the link below are some pictures that I took with this camera (overcast day)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005199.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/005199.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by ferg (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||tgowens||06-06-2001||11:53 AM||||I use the Sony Mavica FD-91 for my digital photos in my Graphics & Design business.....take a look at  <A HREF="http://www.half.com" TARGET=_blank>www.half.com</A>  - I have seen them there for $550....... it uses a standard floppy disk and the quantity of captures on a disk is dependent on the resolution you set (1024 X 768 or 640 X 480) - I researched many cameras and chose this one because of its simplicity (floppy to PC or even to the photo shop for hard prints.........) If you would like to see samples of some of the photos from this camera you may look at my West Highland Graphics & Design site  <A HREF="http://www.whgraphics.com" TARGET=_blank>www.whgraphics.com</A>  or my home  page  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/my_favorite_photos.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tgowens.com/my_favorite_photos.html</A>  <P>I hope this helps.......<P>Terry<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/3GTS_SIG.gif">                 </A>||||reg||1
Z||000023||stsmithgt||06-06-2001||12:33 PM||||<B>What was the price ? How many pic's to a disk ?</B><BR>Depends on what you want out of it.<BR>different resolutions plus different sized memory sticks vary the quantity of pictures per stick.<BR>Did I mention mine comes with a wireless remote?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>25x optical zoom, 450x digital zoom  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR>(use a tripod in digital even with steady shot) <P><p>[This message has been edited by stsmithgt (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||GarethWright||06-07-2001||02:07 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR><B> WHAT !  A 3.4 conversion ??  On a 100% stock Fiero ??   AHHHHWWWWWW  NO NO NO NO<BR>Now I'm going to have nightmears !!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Lol OK OK, <B>I</B> Could get a good start on a 3.4 conversion   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>The canon doesn't run on AA's and I dont think many (if any) do, but it comes with a charger.  Optical Zoom uses the lenses (obviously) but you can get the same quality pic using digital zoom as well, so it has a capacity for 7x zoom(3x optical plus 4x digital),Pic quality depends on how sensitive your CCD is (thats where the Megapixels come in)<BR>Let us know what ya come up with!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||CoolBlue87GT||06-07-2001||09:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GarethWright:<BR><B>  [QUOTE]Originally posted by CoolBlue87GT:<BR>[b] WHAT !  A 3.4 conversion ??  On a 100% stock Fiero ??   AHHHHWWWWWW  NO NO NO NO<BR>Now I'm going to have nightmears !!!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Lol OK OK, <B>I</B> Could get a good start on a 3.4 conversion [/B][/QUOTE]<P>Oh, I get it, <B>you</B> could.<P>Yes, the Cannon sounds neat.  I'm still in the "Gee Wizz" stage over the technology.<P>Gosh, you mean I don't pull out the photo like the old Polorid B/W cameras?  ha ha<P>I've played with one of the RCA cameras, and have come to realize I need a better one than that.<P>Thanks for the information from all.||||reg||1
Z||000026||84Bill||06-07-2001||10:34 AM||||Award winning 100% stock super low milage BLUE 87 GT no less! hehe<BR>I have found that most digitals run off AA bats and my Kodak even has an external A/C D/C adapter plug in it! Great stuff huh dave. you can really save a bundle in processing charges, I'v take over 1000 pictures with my Digi and with rechargeable batteries I havnt spent a dime for any pictures so far! LOL I still have undeveloped film from 3 years ago rolling around the junk drawer! no fiero pics on them, low priority stuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>Be sure to get one with Flash stick or mini flash card 2m pixel or higher and external paralel card reader for fast transfers to your PC.<BR>Good luck||||reg||14
Z||000027||WampusCat||06-07-2001||10:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>Be sure to get one with Flash stick or mini flash card 2m pixel or higher and external paralel card reader for fast transfers to your PC.<BR>Good luck</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Most of the newer ones come with a USB cable that transfers from camera to PC just as fast as a card reader.  Just plug in the AC adapter so you don't drain the batts.  Mine also has a video out cable that I can plug from the camera right into my TV to see a full size pic or 9 at once on the screen.  Also has a slide show mode that will show each frame on the card for a few secs and keep cycling through them.  Neat stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Old Lar||06-07-2001||12:39 PM||||I also have a Kodak DC280.  It takes better photos than my 35mm. Get a minimum of 2 m pixel resolution for good detail.  Kodak has a newer vestion, I think its now called the DC3600 or 3800.  Same camera for less $$.||||reg||1
Z||000029||84Bill||06-07-2001||12:50 PM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR>[b]Be sure to get one with Flash stick or mini flash card 2m pixel or higher and external paralel card reader for fast transfers to your PC.<BR>Good luck<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Most of the newer ones come with a USB cable that transfers from camera to PC just as fast as a card reader.  Just plug in the AC adapter so you don't drain the batts.  Mine also has a video out cable that I can plug from the camera right into my TV to see a full size pic or 9 at once on the screen.  Also has a slide show mode that will show each frame on the card for a few secs and keep cycling through them.  Neat stuff.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hehe yeah? mine does that too but the card reader is much much faster and it was free with the camera<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><BR>got it at <A HREF="http://www.Buy.com" TARGET=_blank>www.Buy.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000030||CoolBlue87GT||06-07-2001||03:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>I also have a Kodak DC280.  Kodak has a newer vestion, I think its now called the DC3600 or 3800.  Same camera for less $$.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I'll check it out.  Thks<P>Save money -  Oh yeah,  that's my style !<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000031||CoolBlue87GT||06-07-2001||03:38 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>Award winning 100% stock super low mileage BLUE 87 GT no less! hehe<BR>I have found that most digitals run off AA bats and my Kodak even has an external A/C D/C adapter <BR>Good luck</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That Sig pic was created by Cliff.  Like it ?<BR>I didn't suggest anything, he made it to show the proper size for a sig pic. I like it.<P>BTW, The Kodak I played with was one of the first models they made a few years ago.  I'm sure you have a better one.<P>Didn't mean to say they were all bad, just that 1st version I tried was a little limited.<p>[This message has been edited by CoolBlue87GT (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||6
